UNIT git_line
LANG js
PRIMARY-SHA256 e142978537632cf19cbb0f7b534edce97496381d204443f466d50efe9b97b9fb
SIG git_line(text: string, lines: number[]): number[]
NOTE default export
ABI text = string (UTF-16; only U+000D and U+000A matter). Each entry of lines = number (IEEE-754 double; -0, NaN and
  the infinities arrive as such). The result = a fresh Array of integer numbers, one per entry of lines, in order. The
  artifact is an ES module default-exporting the function and imports nothing.
ENVELOPE widening (js only, verified on the extension oracle): text that is not a string (null, a number) throws a
  TypeError; lines that is not an Array (null) throws a TypeError; the message is not graded. An entry of lines that is a
  string is used as a property key: "2" answers GIT(2), "02" and "x" answer FINAL.
MECHANISM the package's shape, which realizes R1–R5 exactly: a table whose slot 0 holds 0, a counter lf = 1, then for each
  match m of text.matchAll(BREAKS) in order: push lf; when m[0] is "\n" or "\r\n", lf += 1; when m[0] is "" stop. The
  pin's alternation order takes "\r\n" before "\r" (R1). Its `$` has no m flag, so it matches only at the end of text,
  exactly once and last: that empty match pushes the slot of the last parser line (R2, the empty text included), and the
  stop on it is redundant (matchAll ends there anyway). FINAL (R4) is lf after the loop.
MECHANISM TABLE LOOKUP = table[x] ?? lf. Indexing converts x to its property key: -0 becomes "0" (slot 0, answer 0); a
  key that is not an index of the table ("-1", "1.5", "NaN", "Infinity", "1e+21", an integer past P) reads undefined and
  falls to lf. A string entry (the widening) is the same lookup by its own spelling. An explicit Number.isInteger and range
  test is equivalent on numbers. Map over lines with the table built ONCE per call.
MECHANISM the LINE SEPARATOR and PARAGRAPH SEPARATOR are not breaks here and need no handling; if a test text or a
  comment in the artifact names them, write them only as the escapes \u2028 and \u2029, never raw.
PINNED regex BREAKS = /\r\n|\r|\n|$/g CITED R1 R2 — the order CRLF before CR is R1; the end-of-text empty match closes the last line
DEPENDS-ON none
PROVENANCE rederive@4c134cacd842 cli/lsp/pr-review.mjs gitLine (the real code is JavaScript). Language file cut 2026-11-02
  with the primary, before any blind re-emission.
